From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Name the cast's sink after the receiver rather than after the cast

Whatever elects the default output writes its choice into the shared
metadata that every client can see, and the session manager keeps what it is
told. Watched happening: a cast's sink appears, something that is not this
program and not the session manager writes that sink's name as the default a
second later, and the session manager writes it down because that is its
job. Four casts in five minutes left four entries, and the machine's real
output was pushed to fifth place behind them.

A headphone does the same thing and leaves nothing behind. Its name comes
from the device, so the next connection writes the same string over the last
one. Nothing accumulates because nothing is new. The cast's sink was named
from the identifier of the cast, which is new every time, so every cast was
a new name, remembered beside all the others, and never written over.

So the sink is named from what the receiver is called. Casting twice to the
same television now reuses one name and one entry; casting to a different
one adds a second. The list is as long as the number of televisions rather
than the number of casts, which is a list that stops growing.

Nothing is asked of whatever is electing the default and no policy is
fought, which is what the previous attempt at this did by declaring the sink
virtual and worthless - that changed how the sink behaved when it went quiet
and stopped the cast with it. This changes a name and nothing else.

The identifier of the cast still names the client and the pipeline elements,
where being different every time is the whole point of it.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -46,6 +46,33 @@
 
 static GParamSpec * props[PROP_LAST] = { NULL, };
 
+/* The sink is named after the receiver, not after this cast.
+ *
+ * Whatever elects the default output writes its choice into the shared
+ * metadata, and the session manager keeps every distinct name it is told:
+ * a name that is new each time is remembered each time, and a list grows
+ * that nobody reads and nothing prunes. A machine was found whose remembered
+ * output was a cast sink gone for days, with seven more behind it.
+ *
+ * A headphone does not do this, and nothing protects it. Its name comes from
+ * the device, so connecting the same one tomorrow writes the same string
+ * over yesterday's. Nothing accumulates because nothing is new.
+ *
+ * So this is named from what the receiver is called, which does not change
+ * between casts to it, and the list stops at one entry for each television
+ * rather than one for each cast. The identifier of the cast itself still
+ * names the client and the elements, where being different every time is
+ * the point. */
+static gchar *
+nd_pulseaudio_sink_name (NdPulseaudio *self)
+{
+  g_autofree gchar *digest = g_compute_checksum_for_string (G_CHECKSUM_SHA256,
+                                                            self->name ? self->name : "",
+                                                            -1);
+
+  return g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%.8s", digest);
+}
+
 static void
 nd_pulseaudio_get_property (GObject    *object,
                             guint       prop_id,
